1
00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:04.204
Some document types, like PDFs and
images, require models to pre-process.

2
00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:08.608
In this lesson, you'll learn about
document image analysis techniques,

3
00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:11.211
such as document layout
detection and vision transformers,

4
00:00:11.211 --> 00:00:14.781
and how to use these
techniques to process PDFs and images.

5
00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:16.049
Let's get coding.

6
00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:20.186
You have learned so far how to pre-process

7
00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:28.395
However, some documents don't
have structured information

8
00:00:28.395 --> 00:00:30.363
available within the document itself,

9
00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:33.733
such as PDFs and
images. For these documents,

10
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:37.670
you need to use visual information to
understand the structure of the document.

11
00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:41.341
In this lesson, you will learn
about document image analysis,

12
00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:43.443
which allows us to
extract formatting information

13
00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:46.112
and text from the raw image of a document.

14
00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:51.913
In this lesson, you will learn about
two document image analysis methods,

15
00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:54.387
document layout detection
and vision transformers.

16
00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:57.757
Document layout detection
uses an object detection model

17
00:00:57.757 --> 00:01:00.660
to draw and label
bounding boxes on a document.

18
00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:04.631
Once it is drawn those bounding
boxes, the boxes get labeled,

19
00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:08.034
and then the text gets
extracted from within the bounding box.

20
00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:11.271
By contrast, vision
transformers take a document

21
00:01:11.271 --> 00:01:14.340
image as input and produce text as output.

22
00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:17.944
These models can be
trained to produce a structured

23
00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:20.113
output like JSON as the output text.

24
00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.782
As a note, vision
transformers can optionally include

25
00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:25.351
a text prompt just like
an LLM transformer can.

26
00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:28.221
Now let's learn about
document layout detection.

27
00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:31.091
Document layout
detection requires two steps.

28
00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:34.294
First, identifying and
categorizing bounding

29
00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:37.163
boxes around elements within the document,

30
00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:41.534
and then extracting text from
within those bounding boxes.

31
00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:45.338
So for the first step,
you'll draw bounding blocks

32
00:01:45.338 --> 00:01:47.407
around each element within the document,

33
00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:50.510
such as narrative text or
title or bulleted lists.

34
00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.912
Once you've identified and
labeled those bounding boxes,

35
00:01:52.912 --> 00:01:54.614
you need to get the text out.

36
00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:58.251
Depending on the document type,
there may be two methods for doing this.

37
00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:02.255
In some cases, the text is not
available from within the document itself.

38
00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:05.859
In those cases, you'll need to apply
techniques such as object character recognition

39
00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:09.329
or OCR to extract the text is not
available from within the document itself.

40
00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.865
In other cases, such as in some PDFs,

41
00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:14.267
the text is available
within the document itself.

42
00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:17.237
You can use the
bounding box information to

43
00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:19.572
trace the bounding box
back to the original

44
00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:23.076
document and extract the text content
that falls within the bounding box.

45
00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.514
You can take a look below at what the
architecture looks like for the YOLO X model,

46
00:02:27.514 --> 00:02:30.583
which is one of the models that's
frequently used for document layout detection.

47
00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:33.720
And if you're interested,
check out the archive paper below.

48
00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:37.524
For an example of what document
layout detection looks like in practice,

49
00:02:37.524 --> 00:02:39.292
take a look at the example below.

50
00:02:39.626 --> 00:02:43.796
We've drawn bounding boxes around the
various document elements, like the title,

51
00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:45.198
narrative text, and tables.

52
00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:50.003
Once you've identified those bounding
boxes and labeled them, you extract the text.

53
00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.439
Now let's learn about
another technique for document

54
00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:54.974
image analysis, vision transformers.

55
00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:57.744
In contrast to document layout detection,

56
00:02:57.744 --> 00:03:02.482
vision transformers extract content
from PDFs and images in a single step,

57
00:03:02.482 --> 00:03:07.482
whereas document layout detection models
will draw a bounding box and then apply,

58
00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.021
when necessary an OCR model,

59
00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:16.591
In this case, OCR is not required
to extract the text from the image.

60
00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:23.369
One common architecture for
vision transformers is the donut

61
00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:25.905
architecture or document
understanding transformer.

62
00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:29.742
When you're applying a
model such as the donut model,

63
00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:34.147
you can train the model to
produce a valid JSON string as output,

64
00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:36.883
and that JSON string can
contain the structured

65
00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:38.885
document output that we're interested in.

66
00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:43.690
You can see below what the architecture
looks like for the donut model. And again,

67
00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:46.426
if you're interested in learning
more, check out the archive paper.

68
00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:49.829
Now let's take a look at what vision
transformers look like in practice.

69
00:03:50.663 --> 00:03:53.366
You'll take an image
representation of the document.

70
00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:55.501
You'll pass that to
the vision transformer.

71
00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:58.638
And once the vision
transformer has processed the document,

72
00:03:58.638 --> 00:03:59.973
it will output the string.

73
00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:03.710
The string will be a
valid JSON. And in this case,

74
00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:06.079
each element in the
JSON will contain the text

75
00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:08.448
of the element and the
category of the element.

76
00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:11.084
And once we have this string,

77
00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:13.586
we can convert that to the
normalized document elements

78
00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:16.356
that we expect from all of
our various document types.

79
00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:18.658
So when should I use a vision transformer?

80
00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:21.494
And when should I use a
document layout detection model?

81
00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:25.932
Well, each model type has its
advantages and disadvantages.

82
00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:28.067
For document layout detection models,

83
00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:32.672
some of the advantages are the model is
trained on a fixed set of element types,

84
00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:35.842
and so it can become very,
very good at recognizing those.

85
00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:38.444
Second, you get bounding box information,

86
00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:42.215
which allows you to trace the
results back to the original document,

87
00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:45.985
and in some cases allows you to
extract text without running OCR.

88
00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:49.555
The disadvantages include
that some cases document

89
00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:52.425
layout detection models
require two model calls.

90
00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:56.462
First for the object detection
model, and second for the OCR model.

91
00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:02.130
Second, these models are less flexible.
You work from a fixed set of element types.

92
00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:13.513
Some of the advantages of
vision transformers are that they

93
00:05:13.513 --> 00:05:16.182
are flexible for
non-standard document types like forms.

94
00:05:16.783 --> 00:05:21.354
And so they're able to extract
information like key value pairs relatively easily.

95
00:05:21.688 --> 00:05:23.990
They're also more
adaptable to new ontologies.

96
00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.260
Whereas adding a new
element type was difficult

97
00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:28.995
for document layout detection models,

98
00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:32.432
you can add a new element type to a vision

99
00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:33.833
transformer potentially through prompting.

100
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:37.570
Some of the disadvantages are
that the model is generative,

101
00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:41.074
and so it is potentially prone
to hallucination or repetition,

102
00:05:41.074 --> 00:05:44.911
just like a generative model is
in natural language use cases.

103
00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:48.281
Second, these models tend to
be much more computationally

104
00:05:48.281 --> 00:05:50.116
expensive than document
layout detection models,

105
00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:54.687
and so they either require a lot of
computing power or they run more slowly.

106
00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:58.257
Okay, now that we've
learned about some techniques

107
00:05:58.257 --> 00:05:58.591
for pre-processing PDFs and images,

108
00:06:02.295 --> 00:06:05.832
In this exercise, you'll
pre-process the same document first

109
00:06:05.832 --> 00:06:09.769
in an HTML representation and
then in a PDF representation.

110
00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:14.474
And you'll see how you can extract
a similar set of document types,

111
00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:17.343
whether you're processing the
document using a rules-based technique,

112
00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:20.213
or you're extracting that
information based on visual cues.

113
00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:21.514
So let's get started.

114
00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:25.151
First, you'll need to import
some of the same dependencies

115
00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:27.253
that you've imported
in the previous lesson.

116
00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:31.958
So we won't spend a lot of
time talking about these,

117
00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:35.027
only to say since processing
PDFs are model-based workloads,

118
00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:39.766
we're going to do that again through
the API since that requires model setup.

119
00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:49.170
Now, let's take a look at the same
document represented as a PDF and as HTML.

120
00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:53.713
You can take a look at what these
documents look like by viewing the links below.

121
00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:58.918
In this case, the document
is a news article about the

122
00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:02.622
El Niño weather pattern
that was published in CNN.

123
00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:07.126
First, you can process
the document as HTML.

124
00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:12.231
To do that, first use the file name,

125
00:07:12.231 --> 00:07:17.231
and then you can use the same
partition HTML function from the unstructured

126
00:07:18.004 --> 00:07:21.974
open source library that
you used in previous lessons.

127
00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:27.747
So if we pass the file name into that
function, we can get the HTML elements.

128
00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:31.984
Now you can take a look at those elements.

129
00:07:34.821 --> 00:07:39.525
As you can see, it's identified some
narrative text, it's identified some titles,

130
00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:43.196
in this case, using the
HTML tags and some natural

131
00:07:43.196 --> 00:07:45.364
language information
about the text itself.

132
00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:46.966
Now you can pre-process the PDF
representation of the same document. First,

133
00:07:57.076 --> 00:08:00.313
The Fast Strategy extracts text
directly from the document and

134
00:08:00.313 --> 00:08:04.250
can be used in cases of simple
PDFs like this news article.

135
00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.319
And so if you pre-process this
PDF using the fast strategy,

136
00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:21.834
You can also pre-process the document
using a document layout detection model.

137
00:08:25.571 --> 00:08:29.108
In this case, you'll use
the unstructured API to

138
00:08:29.108 --> 00:08:31.844
preprocess the document
using the YOLOX model,

139
00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:34.981
which will draw
bounding boxes around each of

140
00:08:34.981 --> 00:08:38.084
the elements and
extract the text within them.

141
00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:40.953
You supply this
information to the unstructured

142
00:08:40.953 --> 00:08:43.489
API using the high res
model name parameter.

143
00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:46.592
Please be patient since this
is a model-based workload,

144
00:08:46.592 --> 00:08:48.294
processing could take a few minutes.

145
00:08:48.861 --> 00:08:52.198
Once your API call has completed, you
can see what the outputs look like.

146
00:08:52.932 --> 00:08:56.836
Again, you can see that the model
identifies title and narrative text,

147
00:08:56.836 --> 00:09:01.407
and the output is very similar to
the HTML and the fast strategy output.

148
00:09:02.375 --> 00:09:05.678
Once you have these outputs, you can
compare them and see how similar they are.

149
00:09:07.246 --> 00:09:12.246
The HTML outputs have 35 elements, and
you can also count the elements by type.

150
